; Lords2.exe imports DPLAYX.dll BY ORDINAL, not by name (verified with
; tools/net/dpimports.js: IAT slots 0x005CF364 = ordinal 1 and 0x005CF368 =
; ordinal 2). The ordinals are what MUST match; names are exported undecorated
; as well so the DLL is usable by name too.
;
; All eleven exports of the real dplayx.dll are reproduced at their real
; ordinals, not just the two the game uses. That is not tidiness - it is
; required. DirectPlay's service providers (dpwsockx.dll for TCP/IP,
; dpmodemx.dll for modem) each import ONE symbol from "DPLAYX.dll" by name:
; the data export gdwDPlaySPRefCount. Once our proxy is loaded, the loader
; resolves that name against us, and a proxy that does not export it makes
; every service provider fail to bind. Measured: with only ordinals 1 and 2
; exported, DirectPlayCreate returned 0x887700FA (DPERR_UNAVAILABLE) for every
; provider.
;
; The DllXxx entries are marked PRIVATE so they stay out of the import library,
; and they keep their ordinals anyway. MSVC warns LNK4222 about ordinals on COM
; entry points; that advice is right in general and wrong here, because the
; whole point of this table is to mirror the real dplayx.dll ordinal for ordinal.
LIBRARY dplayx
EXPORTS
    DirectPlayCreate       @1
    DirectPlayEnumerateA   @2
    DirectPlayEnumerateW   = Fwd_DirectPlayEnumerateW   @3
    DirectPlayLobbyCreateA = Fwd_DirectPlayLobbyCreateA @4
    DirectPlayLobbyCreateW = Fwd_DirectPlayLobbyCreateW @5
    DllCanUnloadNow        = Fwd_DllCanUnloadNow        @6  PRIVATE
    DllGetClassObject      = Fwd_DllGetClassObject      @7  PRIVATE
    DllRegisterServer      = Fwd_DllRegisterServer      @8  PRIVATE
    DirectPlayEnumerate    = Fwd_DirectPlayEnumerate    @9
    DllUnregisterServer    = Fwd_DllUnregisterServer    @10 PRIVATE
    gdwDPlaySPRefCount     @11 DATA
